  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nRest of Today-Tonight...A few showers are beginning to develop on\nKMLB radar as of around 2 PM. Otherwise, the cumulus field seen on\nGOES-East VIS imagery looks limited in vertical growth. This makes\nsense, given subsidence aloft due to a mid-level ridge over the\nFlorida peninsula. However, additional development (PoPs to\n20-40%) will be possible into the early evening hours. CAMs\nsuggest the sea breeze collision as the focal point for scattered\nshowers and storms through around 7 PM, before drier conditions\ndevelop for the overnight hours. A few strong storms cannot be\nruled out this afternoon, especially along the collision, with the\n15Z XMR sounding finding 900 J/kg of DCAPE. Wind gusts to near 50\nmph, lightning strikes, and locally heavy downpours will be the\nmain threats. Hot conditions, with a Heat Advisory in effect for\nthe entire CWA through 8 PM will yield to overnight lows that\nremain in the mid to upper 70s tonight.\n\nWednesday-Friday...Similar conditions persist through late week,\nas high pressure through the column meanders in the vicinity of \nthe Florida peninsula and the eastern Gulf. While subsidence aloft\ncontinues to be a limiting factor to convection, humid conditions\nlinger, with PWATs 1.8-2+\" providing sufficient moisture for\ndevelopment. Thus, PoPs remain near to below normal (especially \nWednesday), with chances 20-50%. Interior locations will likely \nremain dry Wednesday, though convection overall looks to be widely\nscattered at best to end the work week. Gusty winds to around 50\nmph will continue to be a threat in any stronger storms due to\ndrier air aloft. Light offshore winds overnight remain less than\n10 mph through the day, but back S/SE near the coast as the sea\nbreeze develops.\n\nHumid conditions, limited showers and storms, and a weak east\ncoast sea breeze will maintain hot conditions through Friday.\nHighs slightly above normal in the lower to mid-90s will help to\nsupport heat indices 102-107+ degrees each afternoon. Will\ncontinue to monitor the need for additional Heat Advisories for at\nleast portions of the area through Friday. Regardless, be sure to\npractice heat safety for the remainder of the week.\n\nSaturday-Tuesday...The Atlantic ridge strengthens into the\nweekend, with the ridge axis stretching westward towards the\nFlorida peninsula through the period. Aloft, ridging consolidates\nover the northwestern Gulf coast. Despite ridging weakening\nslightly over the local area, an enhanced pocket of Saharan Air\nLayer (SAL) looks to move into the peninsula. Therefore, PoPs look\nto remain below normal into early next week. Strong wind gusts\nwill continue to be a threat due to dry air aloft. Also, hot\nconditions will likely continue, with highs remaining in the 90s\nand humid August dewpoints.",
